  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Skills for Economic Growth and Prosperity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Underprivileged  Children&#x2019;s Educational Programs (UCEP) is a Bangladeshi NGO that provides pathways through education into vocational skills training and job placement for poor children and adolescents in urban areas, particularly those who have dropped out of or never  been enrolled. UCEP&#x2019;s innovative skills development program is highly successful, reporting a 90% success rate of graduates finding employment. The investment is aligned with DFAT&#x2019;s aid policy priorities and education sector priorities. Australia&#x2019;s strategy for aid investments in education (September 2015) has a specific focus on skills development under pillar 4 Skills for Prosperity, which emphasizes the importance of access to high quality, post-secondary, education and training.  Australia&#x2019;s Foreign Policy White Paper encourages innovation, private-sector led growth and job creation for global prosperity. Building a skilled workforce with equal opportunities for all is an essential element of inclusive development</narrative>
  </description>
